From: DogukanDG <dagtekindogukan1@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 16:22:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Add an arrival calendar plugin

Scylla schedules case arrivals from an inter-arrival distribution alone, with no
notion of when arrivals are possible. Process models discovered by Simod also
carry an arrival calendar -- on BPIC 2012 it covers 75 hours a week, about 45% --
and ignoring it releases the same number of cases across all 168 hours. Resource
calendars still apply, so those cases arrive when nobody is working and queue
until the next shift.

The plugin defers a case that would arrive outside the calendar to the next open
window, reading a new <arrivalCalendar> element inside <startEvent> whose
<timetableItem> children have the same shape as the global configuration's
timetables. Without the element the plugin does nothing, so existing models are
unaffected.

Deferring rather than dropping: the simulated case count still matches
processInstances, which is what keeps runs comparable. The cost is that arrivals
cluster at window openings, so the realised inter-arrival distribution is not
exactly the configured one.

Note on the delay computation. It walks forward over the windows rather than
calling DateTimeUtils.getTimeTableIndexWithinOrNext, which ranks candidates via
getNextOrSameZonedDateTime and can therefore select a window whose start already
passed earlier the same day. That yields a negative duration and lets arrivals
through outside the calendar -- observed as arrivals at 21:00 and 22:00 on
Mondays against a calendar closing at 20:00. The same weakness may affect other
callers of that helper, which is worth a separate look.

Measured against Prosimos on BPIC 2012 at 500 cases: mean cycle time was 2.24x
Prosimos without the calendar and 0.89x with it.
